<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Modena</h1><p class="st-meta">1135 CE – 1288 CE (approx.) · capital Modena</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Emilian city-republic whose Romanesque cathedral is a masterpiece of medieval sculpture, and Bologna's lasting rival on the Via Emilia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> had belonged to the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Canossa</a>, and its cathedral was begun in 1099 by the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanfranco-architect">Lanfranco</a> with sculpture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiligelmo">Wiligelmo</a>. After Matilda's death the citizens organised a commune, with consuls recorded by 1135.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commune was governed by consuls, later a podestà and popular councils. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> was usually Ghibelline and fought a long rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Guelph</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-bologna">Bologna</a> over borders and water rights in the plain. Its cathedral and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghirlandina">Ghirlandina</a> bell tower became symbols of civic identity. Factional wars between noble families, the Grasolfi and Aigoni, crippled the government in the later 13th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1288 the city gave lordship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obizzo-ii-deste">Obizzo II d'Este</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-ferrara">Duchy of Ferrara</a>. Apart from brief interruptions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> from then on, and it later became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1135 CE – 1288 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.29%;width:3.04%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1135 CE – 1288 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Modena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Emilian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Modena (commune)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1212&amp;focus=commune-of-modena" aria-label="Commune of Modena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4941 599 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Modena lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5261 855L5277 849"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5277 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Modena. <a href="/atlas?year=1212&amp;focus=commune-of-modena">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

